.TH CBMAP_DECLARE 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_declare \- declare an ordered map handle and its companion type variables
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_declare(hm_name, key_t, val_t);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares an ordered map handle with the name hm_name. It also declares the
companion type variables. Those variables carry key_t and val_t to every
later type-inferred macro. The macro creates nothing. The handle holds no map
until one of the init macros runs.

This is the declare half of a declare-then-create pair. cbmap_init(3),
cbmap_init_mp(3), cbmap_init_cc(3) and cbmap_init_full(3) are the matching
create half. cbmap_construct(3) and its variants do both steps at one time.
They are the shorter way to write the common case. The two steps stay
separate for a handle that one place declares and another place creates. An
example is a map whose comparison procedure comes from a condition. Another
example is a map that the code creates only after the enclosing function
validates its arguments.

The macro leaves the handle and the companion type variables uninitialized.
The handle therefore holds no usable value before an init macro assigns one,
not even NULL. cbmap_declare_scoped(3) initializes the handle to NULL
instead. It also destroys the map at the exit of the scope.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  Name for the map handle to declare
key_t    Key type. It tells every later macro how to read a key. It also
         tells the init macro which order to give the tree
val_t    Value type. Only the type-inferred macros read it. The library never
         gives it to the container
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro expands to declarations and does not expand to an
expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro expands to a sequence of declarations. Put it where a declaration
is legal, and give it a semicolon of its own at the end
The container is an AVL tree. The key type here therefore also fixes the
order of the walk over the elements. cbmap_begin_iter(3) describes that
order
The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle. A function
that receives the handle therefore needs cbmap_redeclare(3) there
The macro destroys nothing by itself. Destroy the map in this handle with
cbmap_destroy(3)
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
Every other type-inferred macro reads the handle. A call to one of them between
this declaration and an init macro reads an uninitialized variable.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cbmap_declare(sorted, int, char *);
if (use_custom_allocator) {
    cbmap_init_mp(sorted, &my_procs);
} else {
    cbmap_init(sorted);
}
int k = 42;
cbmap_insert(sorted, k, "hello");
cbmap_destroy(sorted);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_declare_scoped (3),
.BR cbmap_init (3),
.BR cbmap_init_mp (3),
.BR cbmap_init_cc (3),
.BR cbmap_init_full (3),
.BR cbmap_construct (3),
.BR cbmap_redeclare (3),
.BR cbmap_begin_iter (3),
.BR cbmap_destroy (3)
